{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789503387,"nonce":"CnCxvg8Xf4VzXzrXH1LWJG+OZOo=","title":"Python: the annotation on an empty list literal was ignored","message":"`parts: list[str] = []` bound a list of boxes: `annotated_value` keeps the value's own type whenever it is not the dynamic one, and an empty literal is `List(Object)`, so the annotation never reached it. Every append then boxed its string, and a join copied all of them back out through `zb_list_from_any_str`. On bench_tokenize with that one annotation the build phase goes from 18 to 8 ms and the join from 3.9 to 2.5, and the collector no longer marks the dead list of pieces (its second collection reached 31 MB in 15 ms; now 9 MB in 5 ms).\n\nAn empty literal has no element to contradict the annotation, so it takes the annotation's element kind, in the typer and the lowering both. Inferring the kind from the appends when there is no annotation is the unification in 202606d.","files":null}]}